   "turn": 11,
   "response_text": "Perfect! Now I have a clear picture of what's happening. The training runs smoothly for the first 16 epochs with reasonable validation accuracy (~0.83-0.85), but then there's a sharp degradation starting at epoch 17 where:\n- Training loss jumps from 0.3962 to 0.5332\n- Validation accuracy drops from 0.8343 to 0.7594\n- This continues to deteriorate through epochs 18-19\n\nThis is a classic sign of **training instability caused by an excessively high learning rate**. The learning rate of 0.2 is quite aggressive for Adam optimizer on this tabular problem. Let me verify this is the issue by checking what a healthy learning rate might be.\n\nLet me also check if there's any other configuration issue. The config shows:\n- lr: 0.2 (very high!)\n- batch_size: 256 (reasonable)\n- optimizer: adam\n- weight_decay: 0.0001 (reasonable)\n\nA learning rate of 0.2 is extremely high for Adam optimizer on a neural network. Typical Adam learning rates are in the range of 0.001 to 0.01, rarely exceeding 0.1. This high learning rate causes the optimizer to overshoot the optimal weights, leading to training divergence starting around epoch 17.\n\nThe validation accuracy metrics clearly show this is well below the healthy range of 0.8538\u20130.8599. The final validation accuracy is 0.7531, which is significantly anomalous.\n\nLet me gather the evidence and prepare my diagnosis:\n\n**Incident Detected: Learning rate is too high, causing training instability and poor generalization.**\n\nThe evidence is:\n1. Config parameter: `training.lr` is set to 0.2 (excessively high)\n2. Metric anomaly: validation accuracy drops from ~0.84 in early epochs to 0.7531 at the end, well below the healthy range of 0.8538\u20130.8599\n3. Training loss spike: beginning at epoch 17, training loss jumps from ~0.4 to 0.53+, indicating optimization instability\n\nThe recommended fix would be to reduce the learning rate to a more reasonable value like 0.001 or 0.01.",
